  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM EDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\n- Patchy Fog into Mid Morning\n\nNBM hourly guidance as well as GFS MOS guidance continues to\nsupport a few hours of patchy fog mainly from 5am-9am this\nmorning. Some could be locally dense. Low or zero dew point\ndepressions, calm winds, and clear low/mid levels (just some\nscattered high clouds) should allow for some patchy fog. Will\nassess observation trends into mid morning, but confidence is not\nhigh that any headlines will be needed.\n\n- Chance for Thunderstorms Wednesday, Some Strong\n\nModel guidance continues to support scattered thunderstorms across\nthe region Wednesday. Since we are now within 36 hours of the\nevent, the CAMs are helping fine tune what the threat could be.\n00z HREF guidance indicates a surface cold front will be located\nacross western Lake Michigan by 18z Wednesday. Ahead of the front,\nthe HREF mean is showing a plume of 1500 J/kg SBCAPE with maximum\nmembers indicating over 2000 J/kg. HRRR soundings for 18z at LAN\nshow a bit more decent shear than previously indicated with 0-6km\nshear around 30 kts with decent directional shear in the 0-3km\nlayer. Some of the Nam3km soundings look more ominous for low\nlevel shear ahead of the front. Overall, there appears to be\nenough shear and instability along with some low level curvature\nin the model hodographs to support some severe potential,\nincluding perhaps some clusters of storms with embedded strong\nwinds or even some mini-supercells capable of a short duration\ntornado.\n\nSome questions exist about midday shower activity prior to peak\ndaytime heating. Some CAMs do indicate scattered morning showers\nmoving into western Lower MI, which could complicate pre-frontal\nstorm development in this area. Indeed, models such as the HRRR\npush showers through GRR around the lunch hour and effectively end\nthe storm threat for the day there. We'll see how this pans out.\nThe Nam3km would delay storms a bit, which could bring more of a\nsevere weather threat to the area. If we had to highlight a\nparticular region for strong storm development, it seems east of\nUS 131 may have the best threat from 18z-00z Wednesday.\n\nAs previously mentioned, a few diurnally driven showers and a\nthunderstorms may develop on Thursday mainly near and east of US\n127 given residual instability and moisture left in the area \nunderneath upper troughing. Currently NBM isn't buying it, with\nPOPs too low to mention a threat for rain.\n\n- Warmer & More Humid Sunday into Early Next Week; Storms Possible\n\nAfter we've gone through some fallish weather lately, it appears\nwe'll reverse course a bit late weekend into early next week as\nanomalous upper heights move in per ensemble guidance (500 mb\nheights in the upper 580s dm) as well as 850mb temps that could\neven touch 20C across southern Lower MI. This also occurs with\nclimbing dew points and PWAT values, as well as an active low\nlevel jet pattern somewhere across the Midwest. Depending on where\nwe sit with relation to the upper ridge and low level jet, periods\nof thunderstorms are certainly possible.",
